A||||16||LZeitgeist||'87 vs. '88 Flywheel Question...||||1||
Z||000000||LZeitgeist||11-17-2001||01:10 AM||||Can a flywheel from an '87 V6 engine be used properly and permanently on an '88 V6 engine, or are there balancing issues (or anything else) that differ between the two?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||11-17-2001||01:11 AM||||No - the '88 is internally balanced which means it uses a neutrally balanced flywheel - the '87 back uses a balanced flywheel.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||watts||11-17-2001||04:45 AM||||If you're REALLY screwed for finding a flywheel - take the '87 one down to a good machine shop and just have it rebalanced to a neutral state.  Had a guy or two in the club here do that.<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||11-17-2001||09:14 AM||||tgowens - thanks - that's what I'd heard, but I wanted to doublecheck before I went and screwed up anyway... Formula88 gets bonus points for being right again.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>watts - hey, that's an *excellent* idea... a good option if needed. <BR>The flywheel on my '88 is still good (as far as I know), but we have an '87 V6 here waiting to go into my Dad's Coupe, but we figured we could save time by getting the one off the '87 refinished and when it comes time to do the clutch swap, use the already-refinished flywheel in place of the one that's already in there so we didn't have to worry about stopping the procedure while we take the '88 flywheel down somewhere and hope they can refinish it for us on the spot... <P>However, you guys saved my butt from making what sounds like a MAJOR faux pas... THANKS!!<P><BR>EDIT - typos<P><p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 11-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||11-17-2001||02:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>...while we take the '88 flywheel down somewhere and hope they can refinish it for us on the spot... <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Make sure you shim the flywheel the same amount that was removed in the resurfacing procedure.  It helps maintain the proper engagement points, etc.  A lot of Fiero clutch issues can be attributed to flywheels that are too "thin".  Just some food for thought.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||11-17-2001||02:13 PM||||Does anybody know the thickness of a "new" flywheel? I need to know for a friends Caddy V8/aluminum flywheel.<BR>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000006||LZeitgeist||11-17-2001||02:36 PM||||mrfiero - when they resurface a flywheel, don't they resurface the entire face, including the areas where the pressure plate attaches? Or do they just resurface the contact areas of the disc?<P>(Are you talking about putting a shim between the crank and the backside of the flywheel?)<P>Thanks for the info - hadn't thought about that sort of thing yet...||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||11-17-2001||02:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>mrfiero - when they resurface a flywheel, don't they resurface the entire face, including the areas where the pressure plate attaches? Or do they just resurface the contact areas of the disc?<P>(Are you talking about putting a shim between the crank and the backside of the flywheel?)<P>Thanks for the info - hadn't thought about that sort of thing yet...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>They only resurface the face of  the flywheel (where the clutch goes), so teh flywheel will actually be thinner after a resurfacing.  I know we are only talking thousandths of an inch, but it does make a difference......the Fiero flywheel is already on the thin side to start with.<P><BR>The shim goes between the engine and the flywheel.....talk to a good parts store (NAPA, etc.) about these or see a good shop.  Not every shop will know what you are talking about, but most will.<P>The principle behind it is simple.....if your flywheel is 1" thick and needs to be 1" thick for the clutch system to operate properly then everytime your resurface the flywheel you need to add the appropriate shims to equal 1".<P>If the machine shop takes off .003" of material then you need a .003" shim, etc.<P>   <P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||11-18-2001||04:03 AM||||Sorry, this dosen't ring true.<P>The Fiero's hydraulic clutch is entirely self-ajusting through the entire total range of servicable flywheel thickness.<P>(they need to grind down the entire face, not just a 'ring in the center' when doing the resurface...)<P>But if you remove 0.003", then the entire system will just ajust that much withought it ever mattering.<P>If it starts to matter, then your flywheel is  FAR FAR FAR too thin and needs to be thrown away!<P>PS: just to answer the orgional question again, NO. 88 and non-88 V6 flywheels (and engines) are NOT COMPATABLE! (88 cranks and flywheels are neutural balance, non-88 are external ballance.  They need to go with each other as a set, and cannot be mixed)<P>PPS: if you need an 88 flywheel, I have one out of my silver car.  I think they can be had under $100 though, so grabing a new one would be good.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||LZeitgeist||11-18-2001||09:14 AM||||artherd - thanks for the update... I'm glad I asked these questions, 'cuz I never knew about all these subtleties in the care and feeding of clutches!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I appreciate the offer of the flywheel, but the one in the Formula should still be good - we were just trying to take care of an extra step  in the process before we tore the car apart... sounds like we'll just be taking the tranny apart and carrying the '88 flywheel downtown to get it resurfaced while the car is apart. Not as speedy as we'd hoped, but at that point, might be good for us to have a reason to take a break on it anyway...||||reg||1
Z||000010||lou_dias||11-18-2001||09:31 AM||||Cooter,<BR>Heads up!  Aluminum Flywheel for the Caddy!!!!<P>More info please.<BR>I am going to want one in the spring...||||reg||1
Z||000011||California Kid||11-18-2001||01:22 PM||||Just thought I'd add a note for the Automatic guys. If your putting a pre '88 auto on an '88 engine, in addition to the above, the pre '88 torque plate (has starter ring gear on it) also has a balancing weight (welded to it) that must be removed. Reason: the 88 engine is internally balanced, if you don't remove it you'll have a little vibration that won't be good for the crank bearings in the long run.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfiero||11-18-2001||02:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Sorry, this dosen't ring true.<P>The Fiero's hydraulic clutch is entirely self-ajusting through the entire total range of servicable flywheel thickness....<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The problem is the Fieros flywheel is already at the very end of its range when the flywheel is new!  Any material that is removed will push everything farther away, so that there is not enough throw to completely engage the clutch.  <P>Is it a big deal?  Maybe not initially, but it certainly puts a strain on the entire clutch system.  Add the usual leaking (or crappy aftermarket) slave cylinder and/or master cylinder to the mix and the problem just compounds itself. <P>You guys can do whatever you wish, I really don't care, but ask yourself this......is it really worth the potential problems by not adding a shim?  It doesn't take any effort to add one when everything is already apart, so why not?  <P>Artherd.....I "owned" your Formula for about a week and returned it because the clutch was crap.  Eric had the clutch replaced, flywheel resurfaced and installed one of those cast iron slave cylinders......I had traded him a yellow Formula hardtop with 44K miles (original clutch) because I wanted the T-Tops, but even that wasn't enough for me to live with how bad it engaged/drove.<P>I'm sure you have de-bugged it by now, but for me it wasn't worth the effort (considering I had a perfectly working clutch on my yellow Formula).<P>At any rate, my recommendation is to shim the flywheel the amount that was removed.  Heed my advice or not, that is up to you, but don't say I didn't warn you.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||LZeitgeist||11-18-2001||11:02 PM||||I'm not trying to start an argument, I'm just trying to clarify things... if the entire face of the flywheel is resurfaced, including the areas where the bolts attach the pressure plate assembly to the flywheel, then the clutch assembly would still be the same distance away from the finished face of the flywheel, no matter how much material is removed...<P>By shimming it on the backside, are you trying to prevent lengthening the distance that the throwout bearing needs to be moved in order to completely disengage the clutch? <P>Would mere thousandths of an inch really make that much difference to the throwout bearing/clutch fork assembly? <P>Again, not trying to start an argument - I'm trying to clarify the details to learn not only the best action to take, but what makes that action the best to take... to learn not just the 'how' but also the 'why'...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||SKIDMARK||11-18-2001||11:44 PM||||I've replaced clutches in everything from a BMW to a Toyota and have never even heard of shimming the flywheel. I guess I screwed all those people. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> Doesn't shimming change the starter ring gear alignment too?||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfiero||11-19-2001||12:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B><BR>By shimming it on the backside, are you trying to prevent lengthening the distance that the throwout bearing needs to be moved in order to completely disengage the clutch? </B><P>Yes.  The idea is to keep everything as it was when it was new.  By shimming the flywheel you will accomplish this.  A lot of Fieros already have throw issues (not enough to properly operate the clutch), so removing material from the flywheel only compounds  this problem.<P><B><BR>Would mere thousandths of an inch really make that much difference to the throwout bearing/clutch fork assembly? <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes.  However, most people probably won't notice any difference.  I can almost guarantee you that a Fiero with a new clutch does not drive/feel the same as a new Fiero on the original clutch.<P>I have had the pleasure of owning and driving some very low mileage Fieros......I had an '88 Formula with 280 miles on the clock.  I also have driven high mileage Fieros with bad clutches and, subsequently, with new, replacement clutches.  In all cases none of the replacement clutches drove or shifted or engaged as smoothly as the original.<P>Again, the vast majority of Fiero owners have no idea what the clutch is supposed to feel like, so they can only use the Fiero they are driving as a base.  A "perfectly" operating clutch to one person is a POS to another.<P>If anyone wants to come to Denver I will be more than  happy to give you a first hand comparison......I have access to a few low mileage Fieros and can probably let you drive one around to see for yourself.<P>I have access to a total of 3 '88 Formulas.....one has 114 miles, another has 232 miles and the "high mileage" one has 1333 miles.     <P><BR>Most people have never heard of shimming as it is not a common practice.  That does not negate the potential benefits however.  Not all cars necessarily need this done, so you didn't screw over any Toyota drivers (they screwed themselves for buying a Tercel in the first place!).<P>I'm going to leave it at that.....do what you will.  Do a little research......ask around to as many shops as you can possibly find and ask their opinion.  My guess is the newer shops will at least know what you are talking about and can give you some insight.  The older "grease pits" with Joe the mechanic will likely have no clue as to what you are referring to, but you never know.<P>I'll reitterate that this is my opinion, your mileage may vary, take it with a grain of salt, etc., but think about new ideas with an open mind.....you'll be surprised what you might learn.  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||LZeitgeist||11-19-2001||01:12 PM||||Yup, soaking it all in, squeezing my brain and seeing what comes out. &lt;grin&gt;<P>Thanks for the extra explanation... I very much prefer knowing the 'whys' along with the 'whats' - also makes the knowledge stick better.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||1
